pītakṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par ser po'i skye mched} (Skt. pītakṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of perfect yellow
lohitakṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par dmar po'i skye mched} (Skt. lohitakṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of perfect red
avadātakṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par dkar po'i skye mched} (Skt. avadātakṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of perfect white
pṛthivīkṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par sa'i skye mched} (Skt. pṛthivīkṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of pure earth
apkṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par chu'i skye mched} (Skt. apkṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of pure water
tejaskṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par me'i skye mched} (Skt. tejaskṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of pure fire
lokajyeṣṭhaḥ|{'jig rten gyi gtso bo} (Skt. loka-jyeṣṭhaḥ) the chief of the world
vāyukṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par rlung gi skye mched} (Skt. vāyukṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of pure air
ākāśakṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par nam mkha'i skye mched} (Skt. ākāśakṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of the void space
vijñānakṛtsnāyatanam|{zad par rnam par shes pa'i skye mched} (Skt. vijñānakṛtsnāyatanam) the sense of the perfect intellect
pṛthivīkṛtsnām ityeke saṃjānate ityūrdhvam adhas tiryag advayam apramāṇam|{la lar zad par sa'o zhes steng dang 'og dang thad kar gnyis su med cing tshad med par 'du shes su byed} (Skt. pṛthivīkṛtsnām ityeke saṃjānate ityūrdhvam adhas tiryag advayam apramāṇam) some conceive that the primary element is earth, and that it is immense, all what is above, below and in an oblique situation with respect to it being not different from it
aptejovāyunīlapītalohitāvadātakṛtsnām ity eke saṃjānate ity ūrdhvam adhas tiryagadvayam apramāṇam|{la lar zad par chu dang me dang rlung dang sngon po dang ser po dang dmar po dang dkar po'o zhes steng dang 'og dang thad kar gnyis su med cing tshad med par 'du shes su byed} (Skt. aptejovāyunīlapītalohitāvadātakṛtsnām ity eke saṃjānate ity ūrdhvam adhas tiryagadvayam apramāṇam) others take for primary elements, water, fire, air, blue, yellow, red, white colors and conceive that they not being different from what is above, below, and in an oblique (or horizontal) situation are immense or infinite
trīṇi vimokṣamukhāni|{rnam par thar ba'i sgo gsum gyi ming la} (Skt. trīṇi vimokṣamukhāni) names of the three doors of emancipation
śūnyatā|{stong pa nyid} (Skt. śūnyatā) emptiness
animittam|{mtshan ma med pa} (Skt. animittam) that cannot be defined by criteria
catvāri pratisaraṇāni|{rton pa bzhi'i ming la} (Skt. catvāri pratisaraṇāni) name of those four things on which one may rely on
sarvadharmābhisaṃbodhivaiśāradyam|{chos thams cad mngon par rdzogs par byang chub pa la mi 'jigs pa} (Skt. sarva-dharmābhisaṃbodhi-vaiśāradyam) intrepidity for becoming accomplished in all things (or moral virtues)
arthapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na vyañjanapratisaraṇena|{don la rton par bya'i tshig 'bru la rton par mi bya} (Skt. arthapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na vyañjanapratisaraṇena) the meaning must be followed and not the letters
dharmapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na pudgalapratisaraṇena|{chos la rton par bya'i gang zag la rton par mi bya} (Skt. dharmapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na pudgalapratisaraṇena) one's trust must be placed on religion and not in man
jñānapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na vijñānapratisaraṇena|{ye shes la rton par bya'i rnam shes la rton par mi bya} (Skt. jñānapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na vijñānapratisaraṇena) one should depend on wisdom and not upon cognition
nītārthasūtrapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na neyārthasūtrapratisaraṇena|{nges pa'i don gyi mdo sde la rton pa bya'i drang ba'i don gyi mdo sde la rton par mi bya} (Skt. nītārthasūtrapratisaraṇena bhavitavyaṃ na neyārthasūtrapratisaraṇena) upon sutra of real meaning one must depend and not upon those of modified signification
trividhāḥ prajñāḥ|{shes rab rnam pa gsum gyi ming la} (Skt. trividhāḥ prajñāḥ) names of the three types of knowledge
